# --- secrets -------------------------------------------------------------
.env
*.key
*.pem
service-account*.json

# --- the withheld answer key ---------------------------------------------
# The whole benchmark claim is that the SEC complaint is never in the fleet's
# context. The Dockerfile already asserts it is absent from the serving image
# and preflight checks it, but a public repo is a second surface — so it is
# excluded here too.
#
# These are derived from a PUBLIC SEC document (comp26607.pdf on sec.gov), so
# this is not secrecy, it is keeping the answer key and the system visibly
# separate. If you would rather publish them for reproducibility — so a judge
# can re-run score_run.py themselves — delete this block and re-commit. That is
# a defensible choice; it is just not the reversible one, so it is not default.
complaint.pdf
complaint.txt
ground_truth.json

# --- evidence: COMMITTED ON PURPOSE --------------------------------------
# cold_prompt_result.json, bench_*.json, run_*.json and scorecard_*.json are
# NOT ignored. They are the measured evidence behind every number in
# SUBMISSION.md and LIVE_RUN.md, and preflight hard-FAILS without the
# contamination transcript. A repo that cites results it does not contain is
# asking to be taken on trust, which is the opposite of the point here.
# Checked: none of them contain credentials.

# --- run artefacts -------------------------------------------------------
*.log
*.tar.gz
scorecard.json

# --- python --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
